  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Initial Load**\n\nI\'m currently looking into how `currentUser` is initially loaded within `App.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m examining the asynchronous operations performed at the application\'s onset. The use of `Promise.all` caught my eye, and I\'m dissecting each API call within that array to understand their impact on the loading sequence and, consequently, the initial availability of user-specific data.\n\n\n**Refining First View**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the initial page rendering when a user visits the site. I\'m focusing on the logic that decides whether to show the workspace (if logged in) or a dedicated front page (if not). I\'ve got a handle on the conditional rendering: logged-in users go straight to the dashboard. Guest users get the new landing page, which will feature the login/registration buttons.\n\n\n**Designing Guest Exploration**\n\nI\'ve been thinking about enhancing the front page. I\'m adding options for guest exploration. There will be buttons like \"Explore Live Workspace (Guest Preview)\" or \"Launch Interactive Sandbox.\" These allow guest access with a clear \"Back to Home\" option in the workspace\'s top bar.\n\nI\'m working on the logout flow. The `handleLogout` function will call `api.logout()`, clear the `currentUser`, and reset guest preview status. Instead of displaying a modal, it will immediately redirect the user back to the front page where they can re-login or register. The top right will have login/register buttons.\n\n\n**Crafting Home Design**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the design of `FrontPage.tsx`. It\'s crucial, so I\'m meticulously following the Frontend Design Constitution in `SKILL.md`. I\'m meticulously adhering to the rules, especially those concerning function, legibility, and whitespace. Buttons within the page will trigger the AuthModal in register mode, leading to smooth integration with the workspace upon success. All elements must follow the rules, from layout to component design!\n\n\n"
